    "first_prompt_rendered": "<|im_start|>system\nYou are a Python coding assistant.\nReply in exactly one of two ways.\n1. A single fenced Python code block (```python ... ```) that defines the function named in the task, including every import it needs. Write no prose outside the block.\n2. If you need information from the user before you can answer, reply with a single line that starts with `QUESTION:` followed by your question, and nothing else.\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nTask:\nWrite `running_total(a)` that takes a one-dimensional sequence of numbers and returns a NumPy array of the same length holding the cumulative sum.\n\nDocumentation:\n\n[numpy 2.4.6 | api] cumsum\nnumpy.cumsum\nVersion: numpy 2.4.6\nSignature: cumsum(a, axis=None, dtype=None, out=None)\nReturn the cumulative sum of the elements along a given axis.\nExample:\nimport numpy as np\na = np.array([[1,2,3], [4,5,6]])\nnp.cumsum(a)\n\n[numpy 1.26.4 | api] cumsum\nnumpy.cumsum\nVersion: numpy 1.26.4\nSignature: cumsum(a, axis=None, dtype=None, out=None)\nReturn the cumulative sum of the elements along a given axis.\nExample:\na = np.array([[1,2,3], [4,5,6]])\nnp.cumsum(a)\n\n[numpy 2.4.6 | api] cumulative_sum\nnumpy.cumulative_sum\nVersion: numpy 2.4.6\nSignature: cumulative_sum(x, /, *, axis=None, dtype=None, out=None, include_initial=False)\nReturn the cumulative sum of the elements along a given axis.\nExample:\na = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\nnp.cumulative_sum(a)\n\n[numpy 2.4.6 | api] cumulative_prod\nnumpy.cumulative_prod\nVersion: numpy 2.4.6\nSignature: cumulative_prod(x, /, *, axis=None, dtype=None, out=None, include_initial=False)\nReturn the cumulative product of elements along a given axis.\nExample:\na = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nnp.cumulative_prod(a)  # intermediate results 1, 1*2\n                       # total product 1*2*3 = 6\n\n[numpy 1.26.4 | api] cumproduct\nnumpy.cumproduct\nVersion: numpy 1.26.4\nSignature: cumproduct(*args, **kwargs)\nReturn the cumulative product over the given axis.\nDeprecated since 1.25.0: `cumproduct` is deprecated as of NumPy 1.25.0, and will be removed in NumPy 2.0. Please use `cumprod` instead.\n\n[numpy 1.26.4 | api] nancumsum\nnumpy.nancumsum\nVersion: numpy 1.26.4\nSignature: nancumsum(a, axis=None, dtype=None, out=None)\nReturn the cumulative sum of array elements over a given axis treating Not a Numbers (NaNs) as zero.\nExample:\nnp.nancumsum(1)\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n",
